3| Source of the corrections: p4_20261105/codex_codex_r575_p4.txt (F1-F5) and p4_20261105/DISPOSITION.md.  CLOSURE3.md itself is NOT
4| rewritten; this file sits beside it and supersedes the three sentences quoted below.  Each correction was re-checked against
5| src/cpus/cpu_ppc_instr.c and the probe before it was written here (the seat that wrote CLOSURE3 wrote this too, and a correction is
6| not safer than the thing it corrects).  F2 and F3 were corrected in the probe itself, not in CLOSURE3: see RECORDS4_NOTE.md.
7| 
8| The fix, the four measured rows, the 70-row gate block, table 3 (table3/SCORES.txt, PRETABLE_3.out) and the 453-cell partition
9| (416 detected, 36 equivalent, 1 disclosed) are unchanged by these errata.  No row, seed, instruction or want changes.
10| 
11| ## F1 -- the field-mask "closed over every constant" sentence (CLOSURE3.md:736-737, section PROOF)
12| 
13| CORRECTS:  "The field masks `& 31' (:2730-2731, :2757-2758) are closed over 0..30 by enumeration; a mask with bits above 4 is the
14|             fix (the field is 5 bits), so the mask sites are closed over every constant too."
15| 
16| WHY IT IS FALSE.  The handlers mask RAW shifted instruction words, not extracted indices (cpu_ppc_instr.c:2730-2731 and
17| :2757-2758: `bt = (iword >> 21) & 31; ba = (iword >> 16) & 31, bb = (iword >> 11) & 31`).  For opcode 19 the shifted words are
18| 
19|     iword >> 21 = (19 << 5)  | BT
20|     iword >> 16 = (19 << 10) | (BT << 5) | BA
21|     iword >> 11 = (19 << 15) | (BT << 10) | (BA << 5) | BB
22| 
23| so a mask with a bit above bit 4 imports the neighbouring field (or the opcode): BT mask 63 gives BT + 32 (19 = 0b10011 has bit 0
24| set), BA mask 63 imports BT's low bit, BB mask 63 imports BA's low bit.  Such a decode is not the fix; it can also produce a shift
25| count `31 - bt' outside 0..31 at :2734/:2736.  The simulator cannot see this: v575c.py:94 masks the ALREADY EXTRACTED indices
26| (`bt & V.get("mbt", 31)`), so in its model a mask with bits above 4 is indeed the identity -- a property of the model, not of the C.
27| 
28| CORRECTED CLAIM.  The field-mask sites are closed over m = 0..30 by enumeration only: those 186 cells (m = 0..30 at BT, BA, BB in
29| both handlers) are all DETECTED (table 3 / codex's pass-4 partition: 186 of 186; the BT families on their `setbits' row, crandc BB
30| on `64 crandc bbmask', crorc BA on `64 crorc baread', crorc BB on `64 crorc bbmask', crandc BA m = 0..29 on `64 crandc setbits'
31| and m = 30 on `64 crandc 10').  Field masks with any bit above bit 4 (m >= 32) act on the raw shifted word, import neighbouring
32| fields, and are OUTSIDE the 453-cell denominator: DISCLOSED here, not proved and not simulated (their compiled behaviour, including
33| the out-of-range shift counts, is not modelled).  The PROOF section's six constant-space arguments (the four read masks, the two
34| set literals; CLOSURE3.md:720-735 as corrected by F4 below) stand: they act on `cr >> (31-x)` and `C << (31-BT)`, whose operands
35| ARE the extracted values.
36| 
37| ## F4 -- "C without bit 0 ... red on every result-1 row" (CLOSURE3.md:732, section PROOF)
38| 
39| CORRECTS:  "C without bit 0: CR[BT] is never set, red on every result-1 row."
40| 
41| WHY IT IS FALSE.  CR[BT] is indeed never set, but a table session runs several instructions, and a later instruction's misplaced
42| literal can land on an earlier destination.  crorc set literal C = 2 in the 64-bit table session (PRETABLE_3.out:12: crorc 12,0,2;
43| crorc 13,0,1; crorc 14,1,0; crorc 15,1,3): `C << (31-BT)` sets CR[BT-1], so crorc 14 sets CR[13] and crorc 15 sets CR[14].  CR[14]
44| is the destination of `64 crorc 10' (want 1): that row PASSES although its own instruction never set its bit; so does `32 crorc 10'.
45| The mutant is still red (`64 crorc 00', `64 crorc 01', `64 crorc 11', and the `setbits' row), but not on EVERY result-1 row.
46| 
47| CORRECTED CLAIM.  The set-literal sites are closed by the `setbits' rows directly, with no appeal to the table rows: there BT = 31,
48| the shift is 0, and the final CR is 2 | C on `64 crandc setbits' (want 3) and C on `64 crorc setbits' (want 1).  crorc: every
49| C != 1 is red on `64 crorc setbits'.  crandc: exactly C in {1, 3} pass `64 crandc setbits'; C = 3 is red on `64 crandc 01' (crandc
50| 10,1,0 re-sets CR[9]; CLOSURE3.md:706).  So every C != 1 is detected in both handlers (C = 0 is `set deleted'), which is what the
51| contract claims; the clause about "every result-1 row" is withdrawn.
52| 
53| ## F5 -- the cost of a multi-instruction session (CLOSURE3.md:714-715, "WHY FOUR AND NO FEWER")
54| 
55| CORRECTS:  "So no single instruction serves two families, and 4 rows is the minimum; a multi-instruction session would cost more
56|             rows (session/others/mfcr/disasm), as the edge session shows."
57| 
58| WHY IT IS FALSE.  Under the round-3 whole-CR method a row is self-contained: cr_row (ppc_crlogic_probe.py:374-383; :372-381 in table3/probe70_frozen.py) scores exit0,
59| the register dump's agreement with mfcr r10, and the whole CR in ONE row, so a multi-instruction session costs no apparatus rows.
60| And one such row does serve two families.  Session: seed CR = 0x00000002; crandc 31,30,31; crorc 30,30,0; mfcr r10; compare the
61| whole CR with 0x00000003.  Correct handlers: crandc 31,30,31 reads a = CR[30] = 1, b = CR[31] = 0 and sets CR[31] (CR = 0x3);
62| crorc 30,30,0 reads a = CR[30] = 1, b = CR[0] = 0, clears and re-sets CR[30] (CR = 0x3).  The crandc set literal 1+2^k, k = 2..31,
63| also sets CR[31-k], a zero that the second instruction (which rewrites only CR[30]) leaves set: red (k = 1 lands on CR[30] and is
64| red on `64 crandc 01' as before).  The crorc set literal 1+2^k at the k = 26, 27 survivors, shifted by 31-30 = 1, sets CR[4] and
65| CR[3] respectively, both zero: red.  With `64 crorc baread' and `64 crorc bbread' this makes THREE added rows sufficient when a
66| session may hold more than one instruction.
67| 
68| CORRECTED CLAIM.  Four is the minimum under the SINGLE-instruction restriction the round chose (the first sentence of "WHY FOUR AND
69| NO FEWER", the four families' incompatible seeds, stands as the argument for that case); with multi-instruction sessions three rows
70| suffice, at no extra apparatus cost.  The four measured rows (`baread', `bbread', `crandc setbits', `crorc setbits') stay as they
71| are: table 3 measured them, and the gate's 70-row block names them.
72| 
73| ## Not corrected here
74| 
75|   - F2 (the `64 crorc baread' printed note "a=0 for every mask") and F3 (the probe docstring's placement of the crandc toggle under
76|     "EQUIVALENT, proved") live in ppc_crlogic_probe.py and were corrected there, text only: RECORDS4_NOTE.md.  CLOSURE3.md's own
77|     classification of the toggle (:684-685, :742, :756) is correct and unchanged.
78|   - CLOSURE3.md:12 ("PROVES the rest of the 2^32-constant space at each site") refers to the six read-mask / set-literal sites of
79|     the PROOF section, which pass 4 endorsed; it is not the F1 sentence and is left as written.

